course: dictionary
lesson: 11150
title: Wildlife
level: primary-5
stars: 2
explain: Wildlife means wild animals, birds, insects and plants that live free in nature, not kept by people. The word "wild" is the clue: wildlife lives its own way, without pets, cages or owners.
explain: Context helps you here. «If you find an injured wild bird, tell an adult who can call a wildlife rescue, because wild animals need special care.» The sentence tells you wild animals need special care, so wildlife must mean those wild creatures.
explain: Wildlife is uncountable, like water or sand. You say «some wildlife» or «the local wildlife», not «two wildlifes». It names the whole group of living things, not one animal.
explain: Think of where wildlife lives: forests, oceans and wetlands are habitats. People protect wildlife in parks and reserves, and they keep their distance so the animals stay safe.
fact: Wildlife means wild animals, birds, insects and plants that live free in nature.
fact: Wildlife is uncountable, so say «some wildlife», not «a wildlife».
fact: Wildlife lives in habitats such as forests, oceans and wetlands.
fact: Useful phrases: wildlife park, wildlife reserve, protect wildlife, local wildlife, wildlife habitat.
fact: Never feed or touch wild animals, keep your distance and take your rubbish home.
exercise: «Taronga Zoo runs a wildlife hospital for Australian animals.» What does wildlife mean here? | Toys and stuffed animals in a shop | *Wild animals that live free in nature | Pets that people feed each day | Only the animals kept in cages
exercise: «If you find an injured wild bird, tell an adult who can call a wildlife rescue.» Why do wild animals need care from people? | Because they belong to the zoo | Because they are always asleep | *Because wild animals need special care | Because they are always very loud
exercise: Which sentence uses wildlife correctly? | *The park has lots of wildlife | The park has two wildlifes | The park has a wildlifes | The park has many wildlives
test: Which of these is wildlife? | *A fox that lives free in the wood | A cat asleep on a sofa | A hen in a farmyard coop | A goldfish in a glass bowl
test: «A seal pup alone on a beach is usually waiting for its mother.» What should you do if you find one? | Pick it up and take it home | *Stay well away and tell an adult, who can call a wildlife rescue if it looks hurt | Give it food from your lunch box | Shout loudly to wake it up
exam: Which action protects wildlife? | Chasing birds to see them fly | Touching a bat lying on the ground | *Taking your rubbish home and keeping your distance | Feeding wild animals so they are not hungry
